# ---- Atmosphere backend configuration ----
# Copy to .env and adjust. The server also has safe defaults baked in for the
# public Supabase values, so it runs without a .env in development.
#
# Production checklist: docs/production.md
# With NODE_ENV=production the process refuses to start unless the Work
# Verification path is safely configured (service role, DEVICE_PEPPER,
# company inboxes, real media backend). See backend/src/lib/productionGuards.ts.

# Server
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=4000
# Sold-path outbox worker. Default `all` = HTTP + claim/run in this process
# (one Railway service). `http` = API only. `queue` = worker + health
# (`npm run start:worker`). Same image either way — see docs/production.md.
# WORKER_ROLE=all
# Structured JSON logs on stdout. debug | info | warn | error
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# Comma-separated list of allowed browser origins (frontend dev servers).
# Include both localhost and 127.0.0.1 — Cursor / some browsers send one or the other.
FRONTEND_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5174,http://localhost:5173,http://localhost:5175,http://127.0.0.1:5174,http://127.0.0.1:5173,http://127.0.0.1:5175

# ---- Public site inboxes (required in production) ----
# Company mailboxes only — personal yahoo/gmail/etc. addresses are rejected
# at boot when NODE_ENV=production. Defaults to jack@jettx.ai.
# CONTACT_TO_EMAIL=jack@jettx.ai
# CAREERS_TO_EMAIL=jack@jettx.ai
# ---- Invite / transactional email (Atmosphere sends these) ----
# Job invites, field OTPs, and team invites need a from-address plus either
# SMTP or Resend. CAREERS_FROM_EMAIL defaults to jack@jettx.ai (Reply-To).
# Resend sends as hello@invites.jettx.ai (verified subdomain) and is preferred
# over SMTP so a consumer mailbox login cannot spoof jack@jettx.ai. Unverified
# Resend falls back to onboarding@resend.dev which only reaches the account owner.
# Publish DMARC + Google DKIM (docs/email-deliverability.md) or receivers junk it.
# Without SMTP/Resend, invites still create share links (copy in UI). In development, unset SMTP/Resend
# automatically writes invites to backend/.mail/
# (set SYSTEM_MAIL_DRIVER=off to disable that sink; =log to force it).
# CAREERS_FROM_EMAIL=jack@jettx.ai
# Option A — SMTP (Google Workspace, SES, Postmark SMTP, etc.)
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_SECURE=false
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASS=
# Option B — Resend API (used when SMTP is unset, or as SMTP fallback)
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_...
# RESEND_FROM_EMAIL=hello@invites.jettx.ai
# SYSTEM_MAIL_DRIVER=log
# Put a public https app origin first in FRONTEND_ORIGIN so invite links work.

# Leftover platform APIs (sales, PM, estimator, computer-use, …) stay in the
# tree but are unreachable in production unless you opt in. See
# backend/src/lib/platformSurfaces.ts and docs/production.md.
# Production default: every leftover surface OFF.
# Development default: every leftover surface ON (local modules stay exercisable).
# ENABLE_PLATFORM_APIS=false
# ENABLE_PLATFORM_APIS=sales,pm
# ENABLE_SALES=true
#
# ALLOW_MOCK_DRIVERS=true is refused at boot when NODE_ENV=production.
# Mock leftover drivers (Xactimate / CRM / email marketing) are fine only
# while those surfaces stay gated off. Do not set this in Railway.
# ALLOW_MOCK_DRIVERS=false
#
# Optional Sentry (no SDK). When set, 5xx and unhandled errors POST to the
# store endpoint. Leave unset to no-op. Do not commit a real DSN.
# SENTRY_DSN=
# SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=production
#
# Permit the S3 media stub (s3.example.invalid) — integration tests only.
# ALLOW_S3_STUB=false

# Atmosphere staff who auto-receive /analytics (no SQL grant). Comma-separated.
# Requires SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY so the BFF can upsert analytics_staff.
# ANALYTICS_INTERNAL_EMAILS=jack@jettx.ai
# ANALYTICS_INVESTOR_EMAILS=
# Internal staff site login uses Microsoft Authenticator (TOTP). Secrets are
# encrypted with DEVICE_PEPPER. Apply 20260821210000_internal_staff_totp.sql.

# ---- Supabase ----
# Project URL and PUBLISHABLE (anon) key are safe to expose to browsers.
SUPABASE_URL=https://ccxatzfsvzetciiwsjlj.supabase.co
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=sb_publishable_4ppzqtXQPeVPuzP8Ant-pQ_MZIPMcGn

# SERVICE ROLE key — SERVER-ONLY SECRET. Email/password login does not need it,
# but PIN sign-in DOES: unlocking mints a fresh session for an already-enrolled
# device, which requires admin privileges. Leave it unset and the PIN feature
# simply stays hidden — the app still works, users just always type a password.
#
# Local / preview signup: REQUIRED for unrestricted "any email" signups. Without
# it, Supabase's built-in confirmation email quota (~2/hour) rate-limits new
# accounts. With it set, the BFF creates users via admin.createUser (confirmed,
# no email sent). Copy from Supabase → Project Settings → API → service_role.
# NEVER expose this to the browser and NEVER commit a real value.
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ---- Device PIN ----
# Server-only secret mixed into every PIN hash. It must NOT live in the database
# — that separation is what stops a 4-digit PIN from being swept offline if the
# database is ever leaked. Generate with:  openssl rand -base64 48
# Required in production; development falls back to a well-known placeholder.
# Changing it invalidates every enrolled device, which is what you want if it
# is ever exposed.
# DEVICE_PEPPER=

# ---- Password reset ----
# Where the recovery email sends the user back to. Defaults to the live office
# origin (publicAppOrigin) + /reset-password — never FRONTEND_ORIGIN[0], which
# is often localhost or the unmapped custom domain. localhost:3000 is rejected
# even in development: that is Supabase's default Site URL and this app's Vite
# server is :5174.
#
# Prefer leaving this unset so Atmosphere mails a token_hash link to the live
# office. Set it only to exercise the local reset page:
# PASSWORD_RESET_REDIRECT_URL=http://localhost:5174/reset-password

# ---- Mitigation Estimator / Xactimate ----
# Which driver reaches Xactimate:
#   mock  (default) — a local stand-in. No network. The whole estimator, the
#                     consent flow and price-list reconciliation all work, so the
#                     feature is fully exercisable without anyone typing a real
#                     Xactimate password into a dev machine.
#   api             — Verisk's partner API. The best option if you have an
#                     integration agreement: supported, stable, and it never
#                     replays a password.
#   web             — browser automation of Xactimate Online. For orgs without
#                     API access. Read the notes on XACTIMATE_WEB_AUTOMATION.
# XACTIMATE_DRIVER=mock

# Partner API credentials. Required when XACTIMATE_DRIVER=api; the driver
# refuses to start without them rather than guessing at endpoints.
# XACTIMATE_API_BASE_URL=
# XACTIMATE_API_KEY=

# Browser automation is OFF unless you turn it on here. Whether automating a
# given Xactimate account is permitted depends on that account's terms with
# Verisk — that is the account holder's call, so it takes a deliberate act
# rather than a default. It also replays a password, which the API driver
# never does.
# XACTIMATE_WEB_AUTOMATION=false
# XACTIMATE_HEADLESS=true
# Defaults target Xactimate Online / Xactware sign-in
# (https://www.xactware.com/en-us/sign-in/ and https://www.xactonline.com/).
# Xactimate Online's DOM is not a public interface. Override selectors here when
# the UI moves, so a break is a config edit and not a redeploy. JSON object,
# keys as in DEFAULT_WEB_SELECTORS (src/estimator/mitigation/xactimate/webDriver.ts).
# Include loginUrl / workspaceUrl when your tenant uses a different host.
# XACTIMATE_WEB_SELECTORS={"loginUrl":"https://www.xactware.com/en-us/sign-in/","usernameInput":"#user"}

# After connect: sync the price list via API when available, or export from XO
# and upload CSV/TSV/JSON. Codes and prices come only from that account list —
# knowledge profiles never invent selectors.
# Server-only key for the Xactimate credential vault. Like DEVICE_PEPPER it must
# NEVER reach the database — a password that has to be replayed into a login form
# cannot be hashed, so key custody is the entire protection. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
# Leave it UNSET and at-rest storage is simply unavailable: users connect in
# session-only mode, their password is used once and never written down, and a
# database leak yields nothing because there is nothing there. That is the safer
# configuration — only set this if you need unattended runs (a nightly price
# sync, a scheduled submission) that cannot prompt for a password.
# XACTIMATE_ENC_KEY=

# ---- Carrier program agreements (SLA) ----
# Where the terms a franchise must estimate to come from:
#   manual (default) — someone reads the agreement and enters its terms through
#                      the app. Needs no credentials and is the ONLY source
#                      guaranteed to match what your franchise actually signed.
#   portal           — pulled from a franchisor contractor-portal endpoint that
#                      speaks the documented JSON contract (see
#                      src/estimator/carrier/source.ts). Review what comes back
#                      before saving it — a portal response that silently became
#                      your binding terms is a bad way to find out an endpoint
#                      changed shape.
#   mock             — representative demo terms, for development.
# There is deliberately NO browser scraper for a franchisor portal: a scraper
# written against markup nobody here has seen would not fail loudly when the page
# changed, it would return plausible-but-wrong contract terms, and an estimate
# built on a wrong equipment cap is worse than one built on no cap at all.
# SLA_SOURCE=manual
# SLA_PORTAL_BASE_URL=
# SLA_PORTAL_API_KEY=
# ---- Anthropic API key ----
# SERVER-ONLY SECRET, shared by both AI features. Never expose it to the browser
# and never commit a real value.
#   · Technician assistant — without it /api/technician/assist still answers,
#     using a small rule-based responder. Recording, dictation, and object
#     detection are unaffected either way.
#   · Ask this job / clip Ask — Anthropic is preferred. When this is unset,
#     GEMINI_API_KEY / GOOGLE_API_KEY still drive the chat (same keys that
#     already read clips). Without any of those, Ask falls back to keyword
#     lookup on the file.
#   · Computer use — this is the server-wide fallback; a key connected in the UI
#     takes priority over it.
#   · Learning layer — supplies the Anthropic arms in the routing pool.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ---- Technician app: voice assistant ----
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# Replies are spoken aloud, so they are capped short on purpose.
# ASSISTANT_MAX_TOKENS=512

# ---- Technician app: speech-to-text ----
# Optional. Chrome and Edge dictate in-browser for free; this is the fallback for
# Safari and Firefox, which ship no usable speech recognition. Any
# OpenAI-compatible /audio/transcriptions endpoint works (OpenAI, Groq, or a
# self-hosted whisper.cpp server). Leave unset and the transcribe endpoint
# reports itself unavailable and the UI hides the feature.
# TRANSCRIPTION_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1/audio/transcriptions
# TRANSCRIPTION_API_KEY=
# TRANSCRIPTION_MODEL=whisper-1
# Largest audio clip accepted for transcription (bytes). Default 25 MB.
# MAX_AUDIO_UPLOAD_BYTES=26214400

# ---- Model provider (usage metering) ----
# Server-only key for the upstream model API. The browser never calls the
# provider directly: token counts have to come back through this server to be
# metered, and a client that reported its own counts could under-report and
# spend our margin. Leave unset and /api/ai/* returns 503 while auth, billing
# and the rest of the app keep working.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# ---- Billing ----
# Whether a client may report its own token counts to POST /api/usage/record.
# Defaults to true in development and false in production. Keep it off unless
# you have a trusted server-to-server caller metering work done outside this
# process — the trustworthy path is POST /api/ai/messages, which bills from the
# provider's own usage object.
# ALLOW_CLIENT_METERING=false

# How credit purchases are settled.
#   dev    — a billing manager can settle their own purchase through the API so
#            the credit flow is exercisable without a payment processor.
#            REFUSED AT BOOT in production: it would let anyone mint credits.
#   manual — purchases stay `pending` until something holding the service-role
#            key completes them (a processor webhook, or an operator).
# Defaults to `dev` in development and `manual` in production.
# PAYMENT_PROVIDER=manual
# ---- Web Access ----
# Lets Atmosphere sign in to outside websites (carrier portals, supplier sites)
# and pull data out of or enter data into them on a member's behalf.
#
# Both values below are required for the feature to work. Leave either unset and
# Web Access reports itself as unavailable — the rest of the app is unaffected.
#
# WEB_ACCESS_KEY seals every stored site password (AES-256-GCM) before it
# reaches the database. SERVER-ONLY SECRET: keeping it out of Postgres is what
# stops a database leak from handing over working logins to other people's
# systems. Generate with:  openssl rand -base64 48
# Rotating it invalidates every stored password; members simply re-enter them.
# Required in production; development falls back to a well-known placeholder.
# WEB_ACCESS_KEY=

# Drives the browser. Get one at https://console.anthropic.com/
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# The browser itself must be installed once on the server:
#   npm run browser:install
# Set this only if Chromium lives somewhere Playwright will not find on its own.
# WEB_ACCESS_BROWSER_PATH=

# Optional tuning.
# WEB_ACCESS_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# WEB_ACCESS_EFFORT=high              # low | medium | high | xhigh | max
# WEB_ACCESS_HEADLESS=true            # false shows the browser, useful when
#                                     # working out a new site integration
# WEB_ACCESS_MAX_STEPS=30             # actions one run may take
# WEB_ACCESS_RUN_TIMEOUT_MS=300000    # wall-clock ceiling for one run
# WEB_ACCESS_MAX_CONCURRENT_RUNS=2    # browsers are heavy; refuse rather than
#                                     # exhaust the host

# A run may only visit the connection's own site. List any extra hosts it must
# reach — a separate identity provider, most commonly. Comma-separated.
# WEB_ACCESS_ALLOWED_HOSTS=login.okta.com

# Development only, and ignored in production: allows a connection to point at
# a site running on your own machine. In production, reaching a private address
# is exactly the request the guard exists to refuse.
# WEB_ACCESS_ALLOW_PRIVATE=true

# ---- Verifier ----
# The second agent. After a run reports success it re-opens the site in a
# browser that cannot change anything and checks the work is really there,
# correcting what is safe to correct and asking you about the rest.
#
# It needs no secrets of its own — it uses the same browser and the same model
# as Web Access, and stays off wherever Web Access is unconfigured. Run
# db/verifier.sql once and it works.

# VERIFIER_ENABLED=true               # false switches it off; Web Access is
#                                     # unaffected
# VERIFIER_AUTO_VERIFY=true           # false keeps checks on demand only,
#                                     # instead of after every successful run
# VERIFIER_CHECK_PULLS=true           # false checks data-entry runs only

# How many corrections it may make to one run before it stops and asks a human.
# Raising this means a misunderstanding gets written into the customer's system
# more than once — one attempt then a question is usually what you want.
# VERIFIER_MAX_REPAIR_ATTEMPTS=1

# VERIFIER_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# VERIFIER_EFFORT=high                # judging "is this actually here" off
#                                     # someone else's markup is the hard half;
#                                     # underspending buys confident wrong
#                                     # verdicts, which are worse than none
# VERIFIER_MAX_STEPS=24               # looking costs less than doing
# VERIFIER_TIMEOUT_MS=480000          # covers observe + repair + re-check
# ---- Model providers (reinforcement learning layer) ----
# See docs/reinforcement-learning.md. ALL of these are SERVER-ONLY SECRETS and
# ALL are optional: an unset key simply removes that vendor's arms from the
# routing pool. With one key set the loop still learns over prompt variants;
# with several it also learns which vendor is best at which kind of work, which
# is where most of the value is.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is set near the top of this file — the whole platform
# shares one Anthropic key.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# GOOGLE_API_KEY=
# XAI_API_KEY=

# ---- Address lookup (job intake autocomplete) ----
# Production: set a dedicated Maps/Places key so Start a job uses Google
# address search (Places Autocomplete + Geocoding). Enable Places API
# (New) and Geocoding API on the key in Google Cloud.
# Do not reuse GOOGLE_API_KEY (that one is for Gemini).
# Without a Maps key, lookup falls back to OpenStreetMap (Photon).
# GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=
# GOOGLE_PLACES_API_KEY=

# Open-weights server (vLLM, Ollama, Together, Fireworks, OpenRouter — anything
# speaking the OpenAI /chat/completions dialect). A local server needs no key.
# This is also where a fine-tune trained on our own exported preference pairs
# would be served from; see `npm run learn -- --export <task>`.
# OSS_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8000/v1
# OSS_API_KEY=
# OSS_MODEL=llama-3.3-70b-instruct

# Override a base URL to point at a gateway, a proxy, or a regional endpoint.
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://api.anthropic.com
# GOOGLE_BASE_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com
# XAI_BASE_URL=https://api.x.ai/v1

# Vendors rename and retire model ids on their own schedule. Override without a
# deploy when that happens — an id the vendor rejects takes that arm out of
# service until it is corrected.
# OPENAI_MODEL_FLAGSHIP=gpt-5
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL_BALANCED=claude-sonnet-5
# GOOGLE_MODEL_FAST=gemini-3.6-flash
# XAI_MODEL_FLAGSHIP=grok-4

# Prices in the catalog are estimates used for RELATIVE cost scoring. Correct
# them against your invoices without a deploy:
#   {"openai:flagship":{"input":1.25,"output":10}}
# AI_PRICE_OVERRIDES=

# ---- Learning loop ----
# Set to false to always serve the champion arm. Runs are still recorded and
# scored, so you accumulate the evidence that makes exploration informed before
# letting it touch real users. A sensible way to start in a new environment.
# AI_EXPLORATION_ENABLED=true

# Share of traffic spent on challenger arms. Exploration is paid for in real
# work quality, so keep it small — 10% resolves a clearly better arm in days.
# AI_CANDIDATE_TRAFFIC_SHARE=0.1

# Observations before a context slice is trusted over its broader parent.
# AI_MIN_TRIALS_PER_ARM=30

# Accepted past outputs injected as few-shot examples. Higher means stronger
# house-style matching and a bigger prompt bill.
# AI_MAX_EXEMPLARS=3

# Hard ceiling on one model call before failing over to another arm.
# AI_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=60000

# ---- Construction Estimator ----
# The estimator signs in to DocuSketch, Dash, and Xactimate on behalf of an
# organization, so it necessarily holds real vendor credentials.

# SERVER-ONLY SECRET. AES-256-GCM key that encrypts every stored vendor
# credential before it reaches Postgres. It must NOT live in the database —
# that separation is what makes a database leak yield ciphertext and nothing
# else. Generate with:  openssl rand -base64 32
# Without it, credentials cannot be stored and only sandbox mode works.
# ESTIMATOR_CREDENTIAL_KEY=

# Reading damage off field photos and scope directions out of CRM job notes uses
# the same ANTHROPIC_API_KEY declared above. Optional: leave it unset and the
# estimator still runs, building scope from the scan measurements and the
# mitigation estimate, and saying so in the run log.
# ESTIMATOR_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# low | medium | high | xhigh | max — raise it if photo findings come back thin.
# ESTIMATOR_EFFORT=medium

# `sandbox` serves built-in fixtures so the whole pipeline can be exercised with
# no vendor account; `live` talks to the real APIs. Defaults to sandbox in
# development and live in production, so a deploy cannot silently serve fixtures.
# ESTIMATOR_CONNECTOR_MODE=sandbox

# Vendor API roots. Every integrator's tenant is somewhere different (regional
# hosts, on-prem Dash, Xactimate vs XactAnalysis), so there is no useful default
# — an unset host makes that connector report itself unconfigured rather than
# guessing. An organization can override any of these alongside its credentials.
# DOCUSKETCH_BASE_URL=
# DASH_BASE_URL=
# XACTIMATE_BASE_URL=

# MICA Dash — automatic drying-export pulls that modify mitigation estimates.
# MICA_DASH_BASE_URL=
# MICA_DASH_API_KEY=

# Outlook drying reports via Microsoft Graph (mailbox search for "drying report").
# OUTLOOK_GRAPH_BASE_URL=https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0
# OUTLOOK_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# OUTLOOK_MAILBOX=me
# OUTLOOK_FOLDER=inbox

# Capture agent — watches open mitigation jobs and updates estimates when
# MICA Dash / Outlook land new drying reports. On by default (agent platform).
# CAPTURE_AGENT_ENABLED=true
# CAPTURE_AGENT_INTERVAL_MINUTES=15
# CAPTURE_AGENT_SOURCES=mica_dash,outlook

# Cost and throughput controls for the photo-reading stage. Each full-resolution
# photo costs roughly 5k input tokens, so the per-run cap is the main lever.
# ESTIMATOR_MAX_PHOTOS=40
# ESTIMATOR_PHOTO_CONCURRENCY=4
# ESTIMATOR_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
# ESTIMATOR_MAX_RETRIES=3

# ---- Backups ----
# The CRM accumulates the only record of a company's customers and jobs, so we
# keep our own copies rather than trusting a single database to never fail.
#
# Snapshots read across every organization, which needs the SERVICE ROLE key
# above. Without it, scheduled backups stay OFF and say so at boot.
# BACKUP_ENABLED=true

# Where archives go. 'local' writes to BACKUP_DIR — simple, but it only protects
# you if that volume outlives the database host. 'supabase' uploads to a private
# Storage bucket instead.
# BACKUP_DRIVER=local
# BACKUP_DIR=./backups
# BACKUP_BUCKET=atmosphere-backups

# How often to snapshot, and how long to keep finished archives.
# BACKUP_INTERVAL_MINUTES=1440
# BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# Take a snapshot ~30s after boot. Off by default so a crash-looping process
# cannot spray half-written archives at storage on every restart.
# BACKUP_RUN_ON_BOOT=false

# AES-256-GCM key, base64, exactly 32 bytes. REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION — an archive
# holds every customer record we have, and unlike the database it gets copied to
# laptops and object stores. Generate with:  openssl rand -base64 32
# Keep the old key when you rotate, or archives written with it become unreadable.
# BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_KEY_ID=primary

# ---- External application mirror / CRM connections ----
# We run the business in other people's software. This subsystem keeps our own
# verbatim, versioned copy of that data so it survives losing vendor access —
# and lets salespeople connect Dash, Luxor, Salesforce, or any CRM from the UI.
# INTEGRATIONS_ENABLED=true
# INTEGRATION_MAX_RECORDS=50000
# INTEGRATION_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
#
# Seals CRM credentials entered through /integrations (AES-256-GCM). Generate:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# Leave unset in development and a placeholder is used; required for production
# self-serve connects.
# INTEGRATIONS_CREDENTIAL_KEY=
#
# Vendor credentials may ALSO be supplied as env refs (never stored as plaintext).
# A source row holds only the NAME of a secret; the value is read as
# ATM_INTEGRATION_<NAME>. A source with credential_ref "ENCIRCLE" reads:
# ATM_INTEGRATION_ENCIRCLE=

# ---- Email Marketing (storm outreach) ----
# Puts CRM contacts on a map, watches weather for storms that threaten them,
# and sends a personal check-in when someone is in the path — then follows up
# after the weather clears. See docs/email-marketing.md.
#
# Weather feed:
#   auto (default) — try National Weather Service; fall back to demo storms
#                    anchored on the org's mapped contacts.
#   nws            — live NWS only (fails the scan if the network call fails).
#   demo           — always use demonstration storms.
# WEATHER_PROVIDER=auto
#
# How outreach leaves the building:
#   log (default) — records the send without calling an ESP. Safe for development.
#   resend        — delivers through Resend when RESEND_API_KEY is set.
# EMAIL_MARKETING_PROVIDER=log
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# From address used with Resend. Defaults to Resend's onboarding sender.
# EMAIL_MARKETING_FROM=hello@invites.jettx.ai

# ---- Cookies ----
# In production behind HTTPS, cookies are automatically marked Secure.
# If the frontend and backend are on different sites, set:
#   COOKIE_SAMESITE=none   (and you MUST serve both over HTTPS)
# COOKIE_SAMESITE=lax
# COOKIE_DOMAIN=

# ---- Stripe ----
# Full checklist: docs/stripe.md
# Setting STRIPE_SECRET_KEY switches billing to Stripe automatically. The
# customer bill is Work Verification (signup Checkout + Settings → Billing).
# Seat/credit catalog endpoints still exist for webhooks; they have no UI.
# Use a TEST secret (sk_test_...) or a restricted key (rk_test_...) with
# Checkout + Customers + Subscriptions permissions. Publishable keys (pk_...)
# are not used — Checkout is hosted server-side.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...

# Signing secret for POST /api/webhooks/stripe, from `stripe listen` or the
# dashboard's webhook endpoint. Without it the webhook rejects EVERY request —
# an unverified payload is indistinguishable from anyone who can reach the URL.
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...

# The webhook needs SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY (above) to write credits and
# payments: it has no user session to act under.

# Create Products + Prices and print link SQL:
#   cd backend && npm run stripe:sync

# Where Stripe returns the customer. Defaults are derived from FRONTEND_ORIGIN.
# STRIPE_SUCCESS_URL=http://localhost:5174/settings?section=billing&checkout=success
# STRIPE_CANCEL_URL=http://localhost:5174/settings?section=billing&checkout=cancelled
# STRIPE_PORTAL_RETURN_URL=http://localhost:5174/settings?section=billing
# STRIPE_ONBOARDING_RETURN_URL=http://localhost:5174/signup
# STRIPE_ONBOARDING_PRICE_ID=price_...   # Work Verification $599/mo (fallback if metering_plan_versions.stripe_price_id unset)
# ---- Project Manager Agent ----
# The writing layer (morning briefs, drafted customer and adjuster updates) uses
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, declared once under "Computer use" below — the two features
# share the one server-side key. OPTIONAL here: without it the brief is still
# generated from the same facts using a deterministic template, and every other
# part of the agent — the rules, the alerts, the generated work — behaves
# identically.

# Which model writes. Prices are NOT set here: public.model_rate_card in the
# database is the source of truth, and metering goes through record_usage.
# PM_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# The background automation pass. OFF by default, deliberately: a timer has no
# user session, so it runs with the service-role key and bypasses RLS — the one
# place in this feature that does. On-demand runs (every page load, and the
# "re-check" button) need no such privilege and work with this unset.
# Requires SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY as well; with either missing it will not start.
# PM_SCHEDULER_ENABLED=false
# PM_SCHEDULER_INTERVAL_MINUTES=30

# Optional dumpster / supplier web-search endpoint. May include {q} and {zip}.
# Without it, dumpster procurement still opens with a deterministic shortlist.
# PM_WEB_SEARCH_URL=

# HMAC-SHA256 secret for POST /api/webhooks/atmosphere-mention. Bridges that
# forward @atmosphere mentions from iMessage / WhatsApp / Signal / SMS must sign
# the raw body. Without it the webhook refuses every request.
# Generate with:  openssl rand -hex 32
# ATMOSPHERE_MENTION_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# ---- Computer use ----
# Lets Claude see and operate computers running the Atmosphere agent. Set to
# false to remove the whole feature — API, WebSocket endpoint and all.
# COMPUTER_USE_ENABLED=true

# The server-wide Anthropic key is ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, set near the top of this
# file — computer use and the technician assistant share it.

# Encrypts each organization's Anthropic key at rest (AES-256-GCM). Required in
# production; development falls back to a well-known placeholder. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
# Rotating it invalidates every stored key — organizations simply re-enter one.
# AI_CREDENTIALS_KEY=
# Where those encrypted keys are written. Ciphertext only, never plaintext.
# AI_CREDENTIALS_PATH=.data/ai-credentials.json

# Signs the long-lived tokens that paired computers reconnect with. Required in
# production. Rotating it unpairs every computer, which is the correct response
# to a suspected leak. Generate with:  openssl rand -base64 48
# AGENT_TOKEN_SECRET=

# Model and screenshot defaults. Quality is economical | balanced | detailed;
# 'balanced' targets roughly 1080p, which trades accuracy against token cost
# well for most work.
# COMPUTER_USE_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# COMPUTER_USE_QUALITY=balanced

# Guard rails — a model driving a real computer can loop, so every task is
# bounded in both steps and wall-clock time.
# COMPUTER_USE_MAX_STEPS=60
# COMPUTER_USE_RUN_TIMEOUT_MS=900000
# COMPUTER_USE_ACTION_TIMEOUT_MS=45000
# COMPUTER_USE_MAX_TOKENS=16000

# ---- Cyber Defense Agent ----
# Monitors every request, blocks hostile IPs, serves honeypot decoys that keep
# attackers away from real data, and auto-patches runtime hardening (rotated
# decoy credentials, expired bans, config audits). Defensive only — on by default.
# CYBER_DEFENSE_ENABLED=true
# CYBER_MONITORING=true
# CYBER_DECEPTION=true
# CYBER_AUTO_BLOCK=true
# CYBER_AUTO_PATCH=true
# CYBER_PATCH_INTERVAL_MINUTES=15

# ---- Video work verification pipeline ----
# Extends proof-of-work with durable multi-stage analysis (FFmpeg frames,
# quality/dedup, Gemini Flash observations, rules engine, human review).
# Docs: docs/video-work-verification.md
#
# Primary low-cost vision model (Gemini). Server-only — never expose to browsers.
# GOOGLE_API_KEY=
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# VERIFICATION_PRIMARY_PROVIDER=google
# VERIFICATION_PRIMARY_MODEL=gemini-3.6-flash
#
# Escalation frontier model (used only for low-confidence / conflicts / safety).
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=   # already used elsewhere; reused here
# VERIFICATION_ESCALATION_PROVIDER=anthropic
# VERIFICATION_ESCALATION_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4-20250514
# VERIFICATION_PROMPT_VERSION=v1
#
# FFmpeg (required on the worker host for server-side frame extraction)
# FFMPEG_PATH=ffmpeg
# FFPROBE_PATH=ffprobe
#
# Storage + limits
# VERIFICATION_STORAGE_BUCKET=job-proofs
# VERIFICATION_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=1500000000
# VERIFICATION_MAX_DURATION_SECONDS=7200
# VERIFICATION_MAX_FRAMES_PER_VIDEO=120
# VERIFICATION_FRAME_INTERVAL_SECONDS=2
# VERIFICATION_TARGET_ANALYSIS_RATIO=0.2
# VERIFICATION_DEFAULT_MONTHLY_BUDGET_USD=250
#
# Customer token markup (Settings → Billing → Token spend). Provider COGS is
# stored as cost_nanos; price_nanos is cost × this factor. Default 10 (~90%
# gross margin on usage). Does not change the $599 Work Verification seat or
# Stripe subscription. TOKEN_BILLABLE_MARKUP is an alias.
# USAGE_CUSTOMER_MARKUP=10
# TOKEN_BILLABLE_MARKUP=10
#
# Local / CI without paid model calls
# VERIFICATION_USE_MOCK_AI=true
# VERIFICATION_ALLOW_MOCK_FALLBACK=true
#
# Hook existing proof uploads into this pipeline (default on). Set false to keep
# only the legacy day-analysis path.
# VERIFICATION_PIPELINE_FROM_PROOF=true

# LLM verifier (primary reviewer — separate from visual observation model)
# VERIFICATION_LLM_VERIFIER_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4-20250514

# Dataset layer rights defaults (off unless explicitly enabled per environment)
# VERIFICATION_DEFAULT_TRAINING_ALLOWED=false
# VERIFICATION_DEFAULT_EVALUATION_ALLOWED=false
# VERIFICATION_DEFAULT_INTERNAL_IMPROVEMENT=false

# ---- Proof / workday video (one object ≤ 24h; fleet = many objects) -------
# Max accepted length of ONE video object in seconds (default 86400 = 24h).
# Fleet retention of billions of hours is many such objects in object storage
# (see docs/media-storage.md and /api/media/catalog), not one longer file.
# PROOF_MAX_DURATION_SECONDS=86400
# Sparse + diversity stills for long files (FFmpeg reads the signed URL).
# Candidates are sampled densely, then near-duplicate frames are dropped so
# a static camera does not waste the model on the same picture for hours.
# PROOF_SPARSE_MAX_FRAMES=180
# PROOF_SPARSE_CANDIDATE_INTERVAL_SECONDS=120
# PROOF_SPARSE_DIVERSITY_HAMMING=8
# PROOF_SPARSE_COVERAGE_INTERVAL_SECONDS=3600
# FFMPEG_PATH=ffmpeg
# Long-form analysis kicks in above this duration (seconds).
# LONG_FORM_SECONDS=900
# LONG_FORM_WINDOW_FRAMES=12
# LONG_FORM_WINDOW_SECONDS=3600
# LONG_FORM_SYNTHESIS_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5
#
# Fleet media catalog / storage driver
# Catalog + twins persist to THIS Supabase project (same as job_proofs) via
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY. Do not set MEDIA_STORE / GEOMETRY_STORE in prod.
# MEDIA_BACKEND=supabase          # supabase (prod) | s3 (stub) | memory (tests)
# MEDIA_HOT_BUCKET=job-proofs
# MEDIA_ARCHIVE_BUCKET=           # S3/archive bucket when MEDIA_BACKEND=s3
# Production refuses MEDIA_BACKEND=memory and the S3 stub unless ALLOW_S3_STUB=true.
# MEDIA_MULTIPART_THRESHOLD_BYTES=67108864
# MEDIA_MULTIPART_PART_BYTES=16777216
# MEDIA_STORE=memory              # tests/smoke only — skip Postgres catalog writes
# GEOMETRY_STORE=memory           # tests/smoke only — skip property_twins writes
# LEGAL_STORE=memory              # tests/smoke only — skip legal hold / activity writes
# Soft default org quotas (unset = unlimited). Override per org via API/table.
# MEDIA_DEFAULT_MAX_HOT_BYTES=
# MEDIA_DEFAULT_MAX_TOTAL_BYTES=
# MEDIA_DEFAULT_MAX_INGEST_SECONDS_PER_DAY=
# MEDIA_DEFAULT_MAX_OBJECT_BYTES=85899345920
